TITLE: CyberPentest: An Automated Penetration Testing Framework Powered by LLMs
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Exploit; Hallucinations; LLMs; Penetration testing; RAG (retrieval-Augmented generation); Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Distributed computer systems; Environmental testing; Network security; Cyber security; Exploit; Hallucination; High costs; Language model; Large language model; Manual methods; Penetration testing; Retrieval-augmented generation; Testing framework; Automation
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity teams face increasing difficulty in conducting timely and comprehensive penetration testing due to the high cost, expertise requirements, and inconsistent outcomes of manual methods. Automated approaches, while promising, often suffer from inflexible execution and inaccuracies, especially when powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) that are prone to hallucinations. This paper presents CyberPentest, an AI-driven automated penetration testing framework that integrates LLMs with a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mechanism to minimize hallucinations and improve test reliability. The system emulates essential penetration testing tasks, including reconnaissance, vulnerability scanning, and exploit simulation, within a controlled environment. Our implementation, supported by a Django-based web interface, demonstrates higher accuracy and reliability compared to existing LLM-based penetration testing solutions. Beyond its technical contributions, this work reflects our journey as cybersecurity students, transitioning from a theoretical understanding to practical system development. By combining domain knowledge with collaborative experimentation, we contribute a validated methodology for secure, scalable, and explainable AI-driven penetration testing. © 2025 IEEE.
